{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"fiendish-communal-cuttlefish","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# Model classes: trivial / standard / frontier as the universal LLM interface\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nrebar's gates deliberately run different model tiers per pass, but the selection is not expressible\nas configuration. It is INFERRED, and the inference inverts off the default provider:\n\n- `review_kernel/verify.py:269` is `verifier_default if model == default_model else (model or\n  verifier_default)`. Any model that is not the literal framework default — every Bedrock id — is\n  read as an explicit operator choice, so the Pass-2/Pass-4 downgrade SILENTLY STOPS APPLYING.\n- `completion.py:332` repeats the same equality test on a separate path, so the completion verifier\n  loses its downgrade the same way.\n- `workflow/gates/code-review.yaml` declares no `model:` on any step, so ALL FOUR of code review's\n  passes take `cfg.model` and it never downgrades at all.\n- `plan-review.yaml:62-65` sets `model_ladder` to BARE names (`claude-haiku-4-5`,\n  `claude-sonnet-4-6`, `claude-opus-4-8`), and `config.py:333-341` maps the prefix `claude` to\n  provider `anthropic`. So on a Bedrock run the Pass-1 finders still resolve to DIRECT ANTHROPIC —\n  a split-brain in the pass that does most of the work (MEASURED on a real review: 41 of 42 calls).\n\nThe root cause is one knob for four passes. `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` is a single scalar, and a scalar cannot\nexpress a per-tier mapping. That is also why `REBAR_LLM_BEDROCK_MODEL_ID` was deleted\n[rebar:281f-3336-c668-4979] — a second scalar does not help.\n\n## Approach\n\nIntroduce THREE capability classes — `trivial`, `standard`, `frontier` — as the universal interface\nevery rebar LLM call site resolves against. Each class is a config SLOT the operator fills with a\nmodel/provider/endpoint (and an optional fallback chain); rebar's internals reference the class, never\na model id.\n\n**Prior art (this shape is not invented).** LiteLLM's proxy config is exactly this: a `model_list` of\n`{model_name: <alias>, litellm_params: {model, api_base, api_key, ...}}` plus\n`router_settings.fallbacks` mapping alias -> [alias]. Callers reference the alias; deployment details\nand failover live in config. Aider solves the same problem role-wise (`--model` / `--weak-model` /\n`--editor-model`, with each main model carrying a default weak model). pydantic-ai supplies the\nruntime half but no config schema: `FallbackModel(default, *fallbacks, fallback_on=...)` takes model\ninstances or `provider:model` strings, so rebar constructs one per class from config.\n\n**Rebar already thinks in these tiers**, which is why the axis is right: `DEFAULT_MODEL` is the\nfrontier model and `VERIFIER_DEFAULT_MODEL` is documented in-code as \"the decisive NON-FRONTIER\nmodel\". This formalizes a distinction the code already makes implicitly.\n\n**Alternative rejected — a second scalar (`REBAR_LLM_VERIFIER_MODEL`).** It fixes only the verifier\nhalf, leaves the Pass-1 ladder routing to Anthropic, and would be a config surface we ship and then\ndeprecate once classes land. Rejected as knowingly short-lived.\n\n**Alternative rejected — provider-qualifying the ladder in the gate YAML.** Most explicit, but it\nhard-codes one provider into the gate definition, which is exactly what makes today's ladder\nnon-portable.\n\n**Config shape.** The three names are SLOTS the client fills, not presets rebar ships and the client\nselects. Nothing requires the operator to know rebar's internal vocabulary beyond the three slot\nnames:\n\n    [tool.rebar.llm.frontier]\n    model = \"us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8\"\n    provider = \"bedrock\"\n\n    [[tool.rebar.llm.trivial.fallback]]\n    model = \"us.anthropic.claude-haiku-4-5\"\n    provider = \"bedrock\"\n\nSecrets are NOT part of this structure. rebar's config layer deliberately keeps API keys env-only\n(`config.py:388-398` states secrets are not config-file keys); LiteLLM puts `api_key` inline in\n`litellm_params` and rebar must not copy that.\n\n**Config discovery is unchanged**, with one addition: `REBAR_LLM_CONFIG_FILE` names a file that\nLAYERS per-key over the discovered config (pyproject `[tool.rebar.llm]` / `rebar.toml` / XDG). It\nlayers rather than replaces so an environment overriding one key does not have to restate the whole\nconfig — duplication is the likelier source of drift than precedence subtlety. No new discovery path\nis invented; the pointer IS the escape hatch.\n\n**`REBAR_LLM_MODEL` is DEPRECATED with a migration window** rather than kept as a permanent alias.\nThere are few clients today and a single-scalar knob alongside a class model is precisely the\nambiguity this work removes.\n\n## Scope\n\nThis parent's contract, delivered by its children:\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/config.py` — the three class slots, their parsing from the existing layered config,\n  and the class resolver.\n- `src/rebar/llm/config.py` / `_config_sources.py` — the `REBAR_LLM_CONFIG_FILE` pointer and its\n  per-key layering.\n- `src/rebar/llm/review_kernel/verify.py`, `src/rebar/llm/completion.py`,\n  `src/rebar/llm/workflow/gates/code-review.yaml` — the three verifier paths resolve `standard`\n  instead of inferring from an equality test.\n- `src/rebar/llm/workflow/gates/plan-review.yaml` and the batch runners — the Pass-1 ladder is\n  expressed in classes, not bare model names.\n- `src/rebar/llm/` — per-class fallback chains constructed as pydantic-ai `FallbackModel`.\n- `src/rebar/llm/config.py`, `docs/` — the `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` deprecation, an ADR, and example configs\n  per provider.\n\n## Testing\n\nUnit tier for resolution and layering (pure config, no model calls); contract tier for the gate\npaths (the observable is which model id each pass resolves to); and one live [operator-attested]\nrun for the end-to-end claim. Per-child tiers are stated on each child.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] every child ticket is closed\n- [ ] a test asserts each of the three verifier paths (plan-review Pass-2/Pass-4, completion) resolves\n      the `standard` class rather than inferring from an equality against the framework default\n- [ ] a test asserts no bare `claude-*` id resolves to provider `anthropic` during a run whose classes\n      are configured for another provider\n- [ ] [operator-attested] a real gate run with classes configured across two providers records the\n      per-pass model ids actually used, cited from the run's usage log in a comment","id":"eb58-e71d-ec90-4b00","parent_id":"061c-ecd1-8967-4a76","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Model classes: trivial/standard/frontier as the universal LLM interface"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785470590359488001,"uuid":"f513e830-860f-43e4-9690-10e61b863e68"}